src/cooldown.ts adapts that project's src/om/cooldown.ts: the {until, reason, stage} entry
shape, the agent-dir-relative JSON file, expiry-on-read, expireCooldowns, and the
cooldownHours: 0 "in-memory only, never touch the disk" semantic -- together with the
in-memory failedInCycle half of src/om/runtime.ts, which is what makes that semantic mean
anything. src/retryable-error.ts adapts src/om/retryable-error.ts: RETRYABLE_ERROR_RE,
isRetryableError, and isStaleExtensionContextError, whose lesson is that a session-replacement
error must never be recorded against a model. The per-file upstream-provenance header
convention this file's siblings follow is also that project's practice. Not taken: its
extractive compile() pipeline, its observational-memory ledger and background workers, and
its settings TUI.

effectiveContextWindow in src/selection.ts, and the ModelTarget.contextWindow field it reads,
adapt that project's src/om/model-budget.ts:15-41 (effectiveContextWindow) and its
OmModelConfig.contextWindow (src/core/unified-config.ts:62-63, "Context window override for
this model. Inherits from Pi's model registry when unset"). The mechanism taken is the
resolution ORDER -- a per-model config override wins over the registry's value -- and the
purpose the dive named for it: let an operator correct a model whose registry-reported window
is wrong, rather than having the context-fit guard skip a target on bad metadata. Upstream's
positive-integer parsing of the field is also followed, which is why 0 is rejected here even
though cooldownHours: 0 is honoured. Upstream's THIRD resolution step, a hardcoded 128 000
fallback, is deliberately NOT taken: this package's whole question at that point is whether a
prompt fits, and inventing a window would let it route on no evidence. 0 means unknown here,
and unknown does not fit.

