src/chain.ts adapts the isFallbackEligible rule from that project's
packages/pi-plugin/src/subagent-runner.ts: advance a fallback chain only on an explicit
allow-list of failure classes, and stop on anything else, so a failure that travels with the
request cannot burn the whole model list. The failure vocabulary is this project's own.

src/collision.ts and test/collision.test.ts adapt two things from that project, neither of
them a line of its code. From packages/pi-plugin/src/fail-closed-pi.test.ts, the
createFakePi() shape: a fake `pi` whose `on()` accumulates handlers into a
Map<string, Handler[]> and whose `emit()` runs the whole list, which is what makes "exactly
one session_before_compact handler" an assertion about the registered SET rather than about
one handler's return value. And the finding that makes the assertion necessary: Pi's
dispatcher runs every registered handler, keeps the last truthy result, and short-circuits
on {cancel: true}, so two owners of that hook are mutually destructive and load order
decides. Upstream records the same collision from its own side, in
packages/e2e-tests/tests/pi-cache-stability.test.ts:576. Upstream's unconditional-cancel
compaction thesis is NOT taken -- it is the inverse of this package's.

This project also adapts a mechanism from neatcontext-plugins:
https://github.com/XTSoftwareLabs/neatcontext-plugins
Copyright (c) 2026 XTSoftwareLabs, licensed under the MIT License.
Read at commit 5b1c750aed83da604c93814081b9daf7267d39f7 -- not from a published tarball.

The suppressor taxonomy in src/selection.ts adapts evaluateSaveNudge from that project's
plugins/claude-code/neatcontext/src/core/save-nudge.mjs: a decision returned as
{fire, reasons, suppressor} with a closed set of named suppressors, so that declining to act
says why and is testable without a host. The suppressor names are this project's own. Its
practice of labelling an uncalibrated threshold as a guess is followed for
MAX_TOKENS_REFUSAL_FRACTION.

src/settings-store.ts adapts the durable-write shape from that project's
plugins/pi/neatcontext/src/core/lite-context.mjs, read at the same commit: fingerprintLite's
optimistic-concurrency token, requireCurrentBase, the mkdir-atomic acquireUpdateLock with its
staleness sweep, replaceLiteDirectory, and -- the load-bearing detail -- updateCapturedLite's
sequence of checking the base fingerprint TWICE, once at prepare and again inside the lock,
for the reason upstream's own comment gives: other writers respect the lock, but an operator
editing the file by hand does not. Upstream fingerprints a whole context directory and renames
directories with a backup and rollback; ours is one settings file, so the fingerprint is a
sha256 of its bytes and the atomic commit is a staging-file rename. Upstream's revision counter
is not taken (pi owns this file's shape). Upstream's 60-second lock staleness threshold is
deliberately NOT kept: ours is 10 seconds to match proper-lockfile's default, because pi
acquires this same lock path through that library and the two holders must agree on when a
lock is dead. Not taken: normalizeCaptureKnowledge's model-authored-path allowlist, which has
no analogue here.

